{
  "dom_id": "step-q170299",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Prove that $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ is odd if $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{n}^{2}+2n-1 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4{n}^{2}+2n-1={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ is {{field-7}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,4{n}^{2}+2n-1\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{n}^{2}+2n-1 & =4(2a+1)^{2}+2(2a+1)-1 \\\\  & =4(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)+2(2a+1)-1 \\\\  & =16{a}^{2}+20a+5 \\\\  & =(16{a}^{2}+20a+4)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(8{a}^{2}+10a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=8{a}^{2}+10a+2$ (highlighted above), we have that $4{n}^{2}+2n-1=2b+1,$ which shows that $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=8{a}^{2}+10a+2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{n}^{2}+2n-1 & =2{{(8{a}^{2}+10a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ is odd.\n\nPROOF\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,$ for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{n}^{2}+2n-1 & =.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ . Since $a$ is , we have that $b$ is .\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4{n}^{2}+2n-1=,\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $4{n}^{2}+2n-1$ is .",
